{"title":"2022 SSMO Accuracy Round Problems/Problem 7","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_SSMO_Accuracy_Round_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>After a robber drives in a car for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> (not necessarily integral) minutes, the car goes at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/3/f436151aea45d2f0027cf21be06a8b4ecfbd94d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120-t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> miles per hour. Whenever the car's speed drops below <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/302e99c1c7cd91a6ae371139e5142ccef5977dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> miles per hour, the robber switches into a new car with no time loss. A police car can drive at a constant speed of 117 miles per hour. Given that the robber starts 1 hour before the police car, how many minutes will pass between when the police car starts and when the police car catches up to the robber?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_SSMO_Accuracy_Round_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>During each hour, the robber travels at a speed of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> miles per hour. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of complete hours that has passed. We have<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/0/1a05f0b0f2004efc2e7002aa4c1300b4fcaa05c4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[117(h)&lt;90(h+1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad117(h+1)\\ge90(h+2)\\implies\\]\" width=\"429\" height=\"18\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/b/c6b4ba76ff45754683baa7ab0a4e5fed4c055f79.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[27h&lt;90\\quad\\text{and}\\quad27h\\ge63\\implies h=3.\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"15\" />Suppose the robber is caught <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/9/8b91b7245b0dd42b4f5585b97e323a8cfaa064a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> minutes into the third hour. At the third hour, the robber has traveled <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/d/95dd2dc39e852b9e637da5f5f14851c1bd84dad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90\\cdot4 = 360$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"94\" height=\"14\" /> miles while the police has traveled <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2c1f62e7286ec705ce7c681ad63414fd0961927.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$117\\cdot3=351$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> miles. We have<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/2/fb26699b18c7df04d4c25ee80c8b6e4ab9404d20.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{120+(120-t_1)}{2}\\cdot \\frac{t_1}{60}+360=117\\cdot\\frac{t_1}{60}+351\\implies\\]\" width=\"389\" height=\"38\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/1/e01a65cb36f1363ab68c4c4deaac92129565c95f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(240t_1-t_1^2)+1080=234t_1\\implies t_1^2-6t_1-1080=(t_1+30)(t_1-36)=0\\implies t_1=36.\\]\" width=\"637\" height=\"20\" />So, the total number of minutes that passed is<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff58b27d5033a8ba522945f4923caaa0f722e0e7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3\\cdot60+36 = 180+36 = \\boxed{216}.\\]\" width=\"238\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>~pinkpig\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018021947\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.009 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.021 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 50/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2471/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:26197:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018021947 and revision id 280151. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}